Yes. But I would change the target slightly:

**Don't try to clone Sega's Football Manager feature-for-feature. Build a simulation with FM-level depth in the systems players interact with most, while deliberately reducing the areas that require Sega-scale licensing, databases, art and manpower.**

A solo/very-small-team project can feel remarkably sophisticated if the simulation underneath it is coherent.

I'd design it as follows.

# Project concept: working title — *Football Dynasty*

**Genre:** Football management simulation
**Platforms:** Windows first → Linux/macOS → Android/iOS later
**Engine:** Godot 4.x
**Database:** SQLite
**Initial price target:** $7.99–14.99
**Business model:** Premium game, no pay-to-win
**Visual match engine:** 2D initially, optional 3D much later
**Core promise:**

> **Every club, player, manager and league exists in a living football world that continues evolving whether the player is watching it or not.**

That final sentence should drive the architecture.

---

# 1. The fundamental difference from a typical "vibe-coded" manager game

A weak football manager game tends to work like this:

```text
Choose team
    ↓
Buy high-rated players
    ↓
Pick formation
    ↓
Random number generator
    ↓
Win/lose
    ↓
Repeat
```

Your game needs interconnected simulation:

```text
PLAYER ATTRIBUTES
      ↓
TACTICAL ROLE
      ↓
TACTICAL SYSTEM
      ↓
PLAYER DECISIONS
      ↓
MATCH EVENTS
      ↓
RESULT
      ↓
FORM / MORALE / FATIGUE
      ↓
PLAYER DEVELOPMENT
      ↓
TEAM PERFORMANCE
      ↓
FINANCES / REPUTATION
      ↓
TRANSFERS
      ↓
AI CLUB DECISIONS
      ↓
WORLD EVOLVES
```

Everything affects something else.

That's what creates the illusion of a living football world.

