# 78. One thing I would NOT do

**Don't tell an AI coding agent:**

> "Build me a Football Manager game with all these features."

You'll get exactly the vibe-coded mess you're trying to avoid.

Instead, we'd treat the project like real software engineering.

For example, development task #1 wouldn't even involve UI:

> Create the deterministic football-world simulation specification and data model.

Then:

> Implement countries and clubs.

Then tests.

Then:

> Implement players.

Then tests.

Then:

> Implement competition scheduling.

Then tests.

And so forth.

Each subsystem should have a **written specification, mathematical model, implementation, unit tests, simulation tests and acceptance criteria before moving on.**

That approach matters far more than which AI model writes the code.

If you're serious about making this your next project, **the next thing I would produce is not code**. I would write the **complete technical design document for Football Dynasty**: database schema, ~40 player attributes and their mathematical interactions, match-engine architecture, tactical model, AI architecture, world simulation, folder structure, Godot architecture, testing framework, save format and exact development milestones. That document can then become the specification that your coding agents are forced to follow rather than letting them invent the architecture as they go.
